{"type": "answer", "run_id": "q14-decide-gate-large-key1b-20261111", "scenario": "eval:q14_decide_gate", "source": "real", "setting": "large", "setting_def": {"piece_chars": 2400, "k": 3, "rerank": false, "router": true}, "drafter": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "qid": "Q14", "request_id": "q14-decide-gate-large-key1b-20261111-large-gpt-oss-120b-Q14", "start_time": "2026-11-11T07:24:20.167+00:00", "degraded": false, "reply": {"request_id": "q14-decide-gate-large-key1b-20261111-large-gpt-oss-120b-Q14", "question": "How does H-Net decide where to cut the byte stream into pieces?", "kind": "answer", "level": 0, "text": "H‑Net determines chunk boundaries with a learned routing module that inspects the contextual information of the raw byte vectors and dynamically predicts where a chunk should end, rather than using fixed‑width or external heuristics[S1].  \nThese boundary indicators are produced end‑to‑end together with the main backbone, so the model jointly optimizes the segmentation strategy while learning to model the sequence[S1].  \nBecause the routing decisions are differentiable (via straight‑through estimators) the network can adjust where to cut the stream during training, compressing the input into semantically meaningful pieces without any external tokenizers[S1].", "sources": [{"sid": "S1", "cite": "2507.07955v2 pp.2-4 §1", "pid": "dynamic_chunking:2400:0004", "paper": "dynamic_chunking", "block_ids": ["dynamic_chunking-p02-b011", "dynamic_chunking-p02-b012", "dynamic_chunking-p03-b001", "dynamic_chunking-p03-b002", "dynamic_chunking-p04-b001", "dynamic_chunking-p04-b002"]}, {"sid": "S2", "cite": "2507.07955v2 p.32 §A.1.3 (appendix)", "pid": "dynamic_chunking:2400:0048", "paper": "dynamic_chunking", "block_ids": ["dynamic_chunking-p32-b005", "dynamic_chunking-p32-b006", "dynamic_chunking-p32-b007", "dynamic_chunking-p32-b008", "dynamic_chunking-p32-b009", "dynamic_chunking-p32-b010"]}, {"sid": "S3", "cite": "2507.07955v2 p.2 §1", "pid": "dynamic_chunking:2400:0002